Date: Sat, 14 Aug 93 13:20:34 -0400
From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
Subject: VC investment strategies

 szabo@netcom.com  (Nick Szabo) writes:
>In my "daydream investments" post I mentioned buying Autocad instead
>of just doing my own startup.  Here's the rationale: Autocad has 
>an up-and-running software distribution & user help system for CAD 
>users.  I want to introduce new kinds of software, not reinvent 
>distribution and servicing.  (put another way, I don't want to add 
>another layer of risk).  Same reason Symantec bought Norton, I think,
>to consolidate distribution, marketing & servicing.

 You don't need full ownership of Autodesk in order to use their distribution
and support system. Why would you pay for more than you need?
 I would estimate that you would need to pay something like a $500 million
premium over the current market price in order to get full control of
Autodesk. It is hard to believe that it would cost more than a few million $
to persuade them to distribute your software as if it were theirs. And most
of the costs and risks of a good user help system are associated with the
need for the staff to have a good understanding of the software that is being
supported. The Autodesk support staff's knowledge of Autocad won't be of
much use in supporting newly created products.
 If you don't want to add another layer of risk, then you certainly don't
want to invest large sums of money in existing products.
 I would be rather surprised if you could demonstrate that Symantec has
done better than the companies it was made from would have done if they
had remained independent. Accountants can easily find some simple savings
that result from these deals, but subtler costs associated with a layer
of management that is more remote from the individual businesses are often
more important.
