 The idea that predicting economic growth accurately is sufficient to
make 25% per year is garbage, but that indicator sounds sufficiently
similar to a number of liquidity measures that good investors actually
look at that I suspect it is of some use in forecasting the market.
 There is some money to be made by approaches like these; if there weren't
any money to be made, what is motivating those who are currently keeping
the market near to a level that reflects most available information? (Or
at least those that do that under normal circumstances; I have doubts
about whether the market is sanely priced at the moment).

>Here are examples of information useful to investors:
[ numerous examples excluding publicly available knowledge of current products]

 One example of how publicly available information enabled me to make money,
contrary to your claim that all such information is already reflected in stock
prices: I bought Chips and Technologies stock shortly after it went public,
because their EGA chip set was cheaper than IBM's (which I believe was its
only competitor at the time). This knowledge was not reflected in the stock
price because very few people were able to know:
 1) what an EGA chip set was,
 2) that C&T's chips were sufficently compatable with IBM's, and
 3) that C&T had gone public at a price that was not unusually high.
 Information that makes headlines is almost certain to be reflected in
stock prices, but information that takes a good deal of effort to acquire
and integrate is often worth the effort that went into getting it.

