Subject: AI: slaves, selfishness, evolution

Fnerd writes:

>> ...The goals were hardwired in by evolution, but the sense of 
>> genetic self- interest has been lost at the programming stage 
>> [...]  The parental drive has become *detached* from the 
>> genetic link and is an independent goal wired into our heads

> [..] Another [explanation] is that parenthood is mostly motivated 
> by "fun and love"--i.e., goals of the meme-organism.

The two explanations are compatible.  New memes are selected for
compatibility with existing selected memes and the original genetic
hard-wiring.  This way we grow and accrete memes which further the
genetic "hidden agendas".

>> ...But how would an intelligent worker ant express this devotion?
>>  Probably in terms of Colony Law Which Must Be Obeyed. [..]
> 
> Evolution evolved self-recognition and immunity to deal with this kind
> of problem.  I'm sure there's an arms race between genetic selfishness
> and memetic recruitment that exploits family-feeling.

Rather than an arms race they collude together.

>> I admit the existence [of] the selfish genetic background which has
>> driven the *existence and construction* of our primal goals,
>> instincts etc, but I don't think that it has any relevance to the
>> *expression* of those goals.
> 
> Hmm.  I have been saying that the genetic goals, for humans, are 
> *not* primary goals.  Reproduction is currently not high on my list.

Reproduction may not appear itself, but where is the sex drive?  And
once children have been produced (which with contraceptives, they may
not, of course or if you're gay - but let's leave these complications
aside - please :-)) I expect your priorities would alter and child
rearing would move up the list (it seems to with people I know :-)

> Our genes program our bodies to hitch a ride on a meme-beast whose
> goals are mostly independent.  I'm flipping my metaphor, but either
> way the memes are mostly in charge and the genes just ask for an
> occasional detour.

Our memes, by our stage of life (self-conscious articulation of goals
and introspection), may be mostly in charge but they are still carrying
out the hard-wired agenda.  Our hard-wiring determined which were the
early memes we found acceptable and took on-board - determined the
original direction our mental bootstrapping through meme space, if you
like.  The genes may only induce the occasional detour now (like the
reordering of priorities upon start of a family), but that's because
they've left control in a "safe pair of hands".

> I don't get what you're saying about expression of goals.

I'd misinterpreted something you said.  What I was trying to say was
that it didn't matter what the evolutionary reason for hard-wired goals
was - they express themselves as independent goals.  So even though all
our basal goals have been generated, via Darwin, to preserve and
replicate our DNA our goals, when expressed, may have nothing to do with
this - eg foster love.

>> Disagree.  Designers of AI slaves are going to be very concerned to
>> produce impressionable slaves - perhaps designed to be imprinted to
>> obey the first master they meet or are sold to.
> 
> My point is that not being able to redesign the master (as our genes
> can) makes the design job harder.  Now you're volunteering to design
> a one-size-fits-all slave, constricting your choices further, making
> it even harder.

Slaves designed for unquestioning suicidal obedience aren't going to be
overly concerned about the specifics of the master - beyond some
identification protocol, that is.

> Intelligence does not exist as a separate capability.  All of the 
> intelligences we know are self-promoting memeplexes.  They are not
> means to ends but treat themselves as their own ends.  Better brains
> would presumably evolve intelligences that were better--at promoting
> their own interests--and harder to bend to other goals.

I agree - but the crux is "their own interests".  If their own interests
(read: goals) happen to be obeying my orders, well, I'm happy with that.

> I guessed earlier that progress on slavery and progress on uploading 
> would follow the same timescale.  If slavery is a harder problem, 
> then uploading will come first no matter how fast the clock runs.

Uploading, depending on what you mean, is not the end of the story. 
Copies of an uploadee will start squabbling, limiting the extent of the
original someone's control.  And copying yourself divides your resultant
successors' wealth by two, reducing the attractiveness of that option
(for some).  Slaves enable larger centrally co-ordinated wealth
complexes to develop.

> All goals are provisional and held in place (or modified or
> removed) by other goals for which they are subgoals.  And the
> supergoals are in turn subgoals.  "It's turtles all the way 
> down," because it goes around.

I agree.  The goals and subgoals and supergoals form and complex self-
reinforcing web.  The task of intelligence is to keep the various goals
in tune with the challenges of the world, generating and modifying
subgoals where necessary.  The more intelligent a being, the more
complex (and hence stable, I guess) the goal web, and hopefully the
better the fit of the goals with the world, and the more effective
action is in achieving those goals.

> Stubborn goals are meant to be thwarted and frustrated.

Not sure what you mean here.

>> I see no reason why life in the service of a master should not be as
>> interesting and stimulating for slaves as life in the Darwinian world
>> is for us. 
> 
> Right, if you can construct a world that the slave can't escape or
> subvert

This implies a will to escape, a goal, which is what we're discussing.

>, and what he does inside it is reasonably profitable on the
> outside for the owner, compared to the profitability of free AIs.

Free AIs may or may not be more individually profitable, but their
wealth will always be fragmenting due to communications problems,
copying etc.  Loyal slaves reap of the benefits of individual
competition within a co-operative hierarchy, since they have goals in
common.  Witness the scientific strides made by the Nazis in rocketry
and the Allies in the atomic bomb under war conditions (when patriotism
becames a comon goal).  They might even out-compete their self-willed
neighbours (the hymenopterous order of insects does quite well).

> The last condition is something our genes haven't had to cope with
> yet--another reason we aren't good examples of viable slaves.

Please explain.

>> Probably a set of slaves wired to obey a particular master 
>> would form a structure [..] like the RC Church or Islam
> 
> What percentage tithe can you expect to get away with?

Voluntary contributions.  My loyal slave-priests would encourage my
slaves to show their religious zeal to the will of their god (me) by
making wealth-offerings, competing with each other.  Perhaps copying
could be controlled by the priests, like the medieval sale of
indulgences....  Slaves would be nominally immortal, but if one of them
laid down their life in obedience to their god their reward in heaven
would be in multiple resurrection from backups.

> -fnerd

