Date:     Mon, 16 Aug 93 10:21:18 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Searle's Latest Book

Today I went to Moe's books and read through the first chapter of Searle's
latest book, "The Rediscovery of the Mind."  It looks great thus far, like
the kind of philosophy book I like - one that challenges the foundational
premises of schools of thought.  He identifies 6 or 7 premises of the
"school" of thought of his opposition critics, and indicates how they might
be false in brief and that he will provide further argument in later
chapters.

Thanks to all those who took up the opposition position to Searle's on
this list, stimulating me to read more of his work!  I'm contemplating
writing a review of it to submit to Extropy or Exponent - if I can't sell
it elsewhere.

Interestingly enough, his first book "Speech Acts" seems to be an attempt
at a theory of language as a form of human action, which sounds intriguingly
like praxaeology.  Haven't read any of that one yet, but I think I will.

Also, it turns out that my USC law prof. friend knows Searle from when she
was studying for her PhD in philosophy of science at UCB.  She says he's
a very smart man, a fairly nice guy, and a good teacher.

Tim Starr - Renaissance Now!

Assistant Editor: Freedom Network News, the newsletter of ISIL,
The International Society for Individual Liberty,
1800 Market St., San Francisco, CA 94102
(415) 864-0952; FAX: (415) 864-7506; 71034.2711@compuserve.com

Think Universally, Act Selfishly - starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu

P.S. - I've also been reading bits and pieces about both Popper and
W.W. Bartley III.  Seems Popper needs to be rescued from his supporters,
and Bartley from himself!

More specifically, Popper apparently never intended falsification to be
taken as a criterion of meaning at all, merely to demarcate the boundary
between "science" and everything else.  Those who take falsification to
be the criterion of meaning are reconstructed Positivists (reconstructed
because the original Positivst criterion of meaning was verification, not
falsification), not Popperians.

Bartley seems to commit the fallacy of self-exclusion with regards to the
criticizability of every theory.

Both of them seem to make the mistake of acting as if psychology were
sufficient for epistemology.

